2. Convert array to list
a) Using Arrays.asList() method
String [] ={"apple","mango","banana"};
List<String> =Arrays.asList(array);
Note: The list returned is fixed-size, you can not add or remove elements from it by can modify existing elements
does not work for primitive such as int, double but works if the array is of objects like String , Integer
As List only works for Objects rather than primitive.

b) Using loop to manually populate the list
 String[] array = {"Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"};
 List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
 for (String item : array) {
    list.add(item);
 }

c) Collections.addAll() method

  String[] array = {"Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"};
  List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
  Collections.addAll(list, array);

Note: This also does not work for primitive, works for objects only

d) mapToObj way
int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
List<Integer> numberList = Arrays.stream(numbers)
    .mapToObj(Integer::valueOf)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

e) boxed way (boxes the primitive values into Objects)
int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
List<Integer> numberList = Arrays.stream(numbers)
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

f) Stream API way
String[] array = {"Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"};
List<String> list = Arrays.stream(array).collect(Collectors.toList());

Note: if the original array is of objects array then this approach is ok, if primitive then need to follow e) or f)
Arrays.stream(numbers) converts int [] array into an IntStream